    "created_at": "2021-12-29T17:57:40Z",
    "updated_at": "2021-12-29T17:59:28Z",
    "body": "I am not sure how to implement it, but maybe one could define `separator_left` and `separator_right`, where the defaults would be an empty string. \r\nHowever, if they are set, you could maybe loop over all modules (specified in `format`) and edit every format. Maybe this helps to understand:\r\n```rust\r\nlet sr = context.config[\"separator_right\"]\r\nlet sl = context.config[\"separator_left\"]\r\n\r\nfor module in context.config[\"format\"] {\r\n   let fm = module.config[\"format\"];\r\n   module.config[\"format\"] = format!(\"{}{}{}\", sl, fm, sr);\r\n}\r\n```\r\nI think this is the best approach, because every module (except `line_break`) has a default format-variable, so we don't even have to check if it exists. \r\nWe can also do it in `print::get_prompt()`, but it has to be before we load the segments, as we can't control how many `ANSIString`s belong to each module.\r\nE.g. the number of `ANSIString`s is different here:\r\n```toml\r\n[my_module]\r\nformat = \"[$fixed_var]($style)\"\r\n\r\n# One ANSIString (with the content of \"$fixed_var\")\r\n\r\n[my_module]\r\nformat = \"text [$fixed_var]($style)\"\r\n\r\n# Two ANSIStrings (with the contents of \"text \" and \"$fixed_var\")\r\n```",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
